 example something  like superman lives which you may recall  was going to be directed by tim burton  with nicholas cage in the titular role  which by the way people often troll  cages look because of this picture but  in the context of the 90s and what tim  burton wanted to do  i'm actually not mad at the way nicholas  cage looks at superman in this picture  but if you're interested in learning  more about superman lives the late john  schnepp directed a documentary called  the death of superman lives that maybe  worth checking out for you there was  also the infamous superman flyby with jj  abrams at the helm you can scour the  internet to find a surprising amount of  details about that film and one idea  that i found interesting was how abrams  wanted to have jarrell  investigating jonathan and martha kent  on earth to see if they were even  suitable to raise kal-el to begin with  and there was also a bit of interesting  spillover when you consider something  like man of steel because abrams wanted  krypton to feel like a fully realized  world with its own language and you may  recall that henry cavill's superman suit  is actually etched with kryptonian  writing which again would suggest that  we got to witness some of flyby's ideas  even if the film itself was never able  to fly by the development process i'm  sorry i couldn't resist but this video  isn't about flyby ladies and gentlemen  this video is about christopher mcqueary  and his pitch for superman which would  have also opened the door for green  lantern in the dc eu as well now when  contrasted to something like superman  lives or flyby we know virtually nothing  about mcquarrie's vision there's no  concept art no pre-production stills no  script leaks  nothing  nada zip zero stingy with de niro the  only thing we know is that macquarie  made a pitch to warner brothers it would  have starred henry cavill tom cruise  would have  99.999 percent played hal jordan  and in fact macquarie and henry actually  proposed the film  together and that'll do it for this  video thank you all so much for watching  i'll talk to you all on the next  editorial no of course this is not the  end did you all think i was gonna leave  you on that anti-climatic note you think  so little of me so we don't know much  about mcquarrie's vision for superman  outside of what he shared in tweets that  have since been deleted but tweets are  like that last jedi quote they're never  really gone  in one tweet mcquarrie says that warner  brothers never cared for his original  ideas and just wanted him to fix their  broken ones he also says that warner  brothers didn't flat out say no they  just never moved or acted upon his pitch  and further mentions that this meeting  happened before the release of mission  impossible fallout which we'll come back  to later  it also appears that the bridge between  warner brothers and macquarie has been  irreparably damaged because he said that  he would not reconsider going back to  superman even if he was invited to do so  now this is where things get interesting  because like i said outside of those  tweets we know nothing about what  mcquarrie wanted to do with superman but  going through his filmography does give  us some interesting insights  the most obvious place to begin is with  this year's biggest hit top gun maverick  which mcqueary received a screenplay  credit for  given how maverick expertly catered to  the nostalgia of fans of the first film  and created a new generation of top gun  fans it's not outside the realm of  possibility that macquarie would have  aimed to do the same with superman and i  wholeheartedly believe he would have  been successful he would have created a  classical superman  and a modern one he would have given us  a classic modern superman what i mean by  that is he would have created an  interpretation of the character that  would have been less contentious and  more broadly accepted than zack snyder's  interpretation  by the way i'm actually a huge fan of  zack snyder and his vision for all dc  characters batman v superman is in my  top 5 all-time favorite cbms if that's a  conversation y'all want to have in a  future editorial let me know in the  comments below because there's people  who are actually lifelong fans of zack  snyder and that film based on my content  and coverage of it in the past i even  had ray fisher and zack snyder on a  podcast just two days after the snyder  cut was confirmed for hbo max  actually now that i think about it that  was zack's  first public appearance since the  snydercon announcement you know what let  me stop flexing and digressing now the  aerial sequences in top gun maverick are  also second to none i'll let your  imagination run wild with all the ways  macquarie would have explored superman's  flight in that regard because i don't  believe my words could even do that idea  or potential justice  which brings us to the mission  impossible series  now if you recall macquarie said that  him and henry made the pitch to warner  brothers prior to mi fallout coming out  there's something i vividly remember  about that film's release henry cavill  during that time on instagram i believe  had under a million followers  because i remember when fallout came out  a friend of mine was monitoring his  account and as soon as that movie was  released that is what we believe pushed  him over a million  and if i'm being delusional and not  remembering correctly his following in  general was relatively low for a guy  playing superman on the big screen but  when fallout came out it went higher  exponentially  higher  and then of course there was a witcher  announcement there was a report back in  2018 about him being out at superman but  then later that day he put out a video  with a caption quote today was exciting  superman then the witcher came out he  assembled his pc in a blue tank top and  the rest is history now let that  marinate for a moment ladies and  gentlemen henry's celebrity has actually  increased not by playing superman  but by  not playing superman so at that time him  and macquarie made that pitch to warner  brothers it's easy to see how the studio  could deny or overlook both of their  respective bargaining power whether  henry wanted more money more creative  control or the combination of both  warner brothers clearly did not take  them seriously and both of their stocks  have skyrocketed since when it's all  said and done christopher mcqueary will  have written and directed four mission  impossible films and coming off the  success of maverick it's highly likely  that mission impossible dead reckoning  will go on to break a billion at the box  office that's obviously going to be  because of tom cruise's momentum post  maverick but again christopher  mcqueary's stock continues to rise  whenever y'all get a chance go through  his catalog of films that he's either  written or directed especially  especially especially  the underrated masterpiece that was edge  of tomorrow with emily blunt and tom  cruise as i was recently doing a binge  of his catalogue it became so crystal  clear to me that he would have been the  ideal director and writer to entrust  with superman he would have nailed the  spectacle the action the emotion and the  cultural nostalgia of the character but  we'll never see it  so let me know your thoughts down below  in the comments after watching this  video do you equally feel confident that  macquarie would have been the ideal  individual to usher in a new era of  superman on film how familiar were you  with this catalog before this video how  intrigued are you by his catalogue after  this video it's interesting because i  was talking to a couple of people about  you know why i wanted to make this video  in the first place and uh their response  was that they didn't even know that  mcqueary wanted to make a superman film  to begin with and that's how i knew that  it would be an interesting and timely  conversation to have  especially when you consider everything  going on currently like we discussed  with warner brothers discovery and the  overall lack of any managerial stability  since the release of batman v superman  back in 2016. but if you enjoyed this  video make sure to hit that like button  and subscribe to geek culture explained  if you haven't already you can find me  on my youtube channel uh which at the  time of this video has uh  seven subscribers and uh zero videos uh  that's like when nba players have zero  points one rebound and six fouls as  their stat line but you know i've been  on this auto didactic uh filmmaking  journey over the past two years and uh  shout out to everyone who watched the  surviving hall h video shout out to rob  gordon and tc for essentially giving me  carte blanche with with that project uh  so i am planning on doing some things on  that channel that i feel could be  important in terms of you know  documenting the journey  of me trying to kind of get my first  film made  because it is a hellacious one and i  feel like every single day of my life i  wake up i feel like i'm either not good  enough or i'm too good or i'm developing  too fast or i'm not developing fast  enough it's a world wind of emotions  across the spectrum so yeah ladies and  gentlemen that'll do it for me um thank  you all so much for your time and i will  see you all on the next editorial

